EMS Triple Slew Limiter

The EMS Triple Slew Limiter is a historically significant, rare analog synthesizer processor released by Electronic Music Studios (EMS) in April 1971. Originally designed by Peter Zinovieff, David Cockerell, and their team, the module pack was part of EMS's premium standalone expansion lineup housed in beautiful, signature Afrormosia (African Teak) wooden cabinets.
It was built alongside three other distinct 1U rack companion pieces—the Eight Octave Filter Bank, the Pitch to Voltage Converter, and the Random Voltage Generator—meant to expand the capabilities of EMS's landmark VCS3 and Synthi A synthesizers.

Core Function & Architecture

At its baseline, a slew limiter sets a speed limit on how fast a voltage waveform can transition from one value to another, smoothing out sharp edges.
• The "Triple" Design: The module packages three identical, independent voltage-controlled slew limiters into one unit. In large-scale systems, these three sub-modules were directly integrated into the massive EMS Synthi 100 console.
• Unity Gain Performance: Unlike standard filters or envelope generators, the EMS design is a unity gain amplifier. The steady-state output exactly tracks the input with high precision (boasting an impressive ~0.05% linearity for the era), meaning it does not alter the absolute voltage level—only the speed at which it gets there. Because of this, the front panel lacks output level controls.
• Exponential Voltage Control: The slew rate can be manually adjusted or modulated externally using Control Voltage (CV). Crucially, the voltage response of the slew control is exponential, which mimics how humans naturally perceive changes in pitch and time.

Technical Specifications

• Slew Range: Highly versatile for its time, the module can smooth voltages from a lightning-fast 1 millisecond up to a massive 10 seconds.
• Linearity: ~0.05% Best Straight Line (BSL) accuracy.
• Gain: 1 (± ~1%).
• Form Factor: Original standalone units were 1U rack-mountable, 110V/240V mains-powered, wrapped in wood.


