1. Active Electronic Components

The original EMS boards (both the standalone rack unit and the internal cards used in the Synthi 100) utilized standard components of the early 1970s British aerospace and audio industries:
• Operational Amplifiers (The LM741 & MC1458): EMS heavily favored early operational amplifiers. For dual-amp configurations, they used the Motorola MC1458 (essentially two 741s in one package). These early op-amps had massive limitations compared to modern ones—most notably a very low slew rate (~0.5 V/µs) and significant input bias current.
	• DSP Impact: The buffers themselves added a slight, natural low-pass filtering and rounding to high-speed edges independent of the slew circuit itself.
• The Logarithmic/Exponential Transistors (BC169 / BC258 or Matched Pairs): To achieve the exponential CV tracking characteristic of EMS modules, the control voltage section utilized discrete silicon NPN/PNP transistors (typically BC169 and BC258, or custom-matched pairs) wired as a logging circuit.
	• DSP Impact: Instead of a linear time coefficient (\(t\)), your software algorithm must map incoming CV using an exponential curve (\(e^{x}\)) to dictate the integration rate.
• The Analog Switch/Current Source Gates: David Cockerell used a combination of discrete JFETs (like the 2N4391 or 2N3819) or early transistor arrays to act as the steering mechanism that poured current into the core integrator capacitor.

2. Passive Components & Flavor Quirks

• The Hold/Slew Capacitors (Polystyrene): The core integrator relied on premium Polystyrene capacitors. Polystyrene was chosen in the 1970s because it has incredibly low dielectric absorption (the "memory effect" where a capacitor remembers its previous voltage state) and minimal leakage.
	• DSP Impact: Unlike a bad sample-and-hold circuit, an EMS slew limiter with polystyrene caps has almost zero natural voltage sag or "droop" when holding a steady-state flat voltage.
• Carbon Film Resistors: 1970s EMS gear used standard carbon film resistors. These components have a high thermal coefficient—meaning as the inside of the rack warmed up, the resistance drifted.
	• DSP Impact: To create an authentic vintage emulation, add a tiny amount of slow thermal "drift" or subtle instability to the maximum and minimum slew boundaries (1 ms to 10 seconds).

3. DSP Mathematical Model for a Software Clone

To accurately emulate the EMS module, you cannot simply use a digital One-Pole Low-Pass Filter (\(y[n] = y[n-1] + \alpha(x[n] - y[n-1])\)), because a standard filter changes its speed depending on how close the input is to the output. A Slew Limiter has a fixed maximum speed limit.

Step 1: Calculate the Dynamic Speed Limit (Slew Rate)

First, map the manual pot and the control voltage exponentially to get the current maximum rate of change per sample:
\(\text{MaxChange}=\text{BaseRate}\times e^{(\text{ManualPot}+\text{CVInput})}\)
